== ARCHIVE: the rebuilt wheel, tested outside the source tree, 45 tests ==

Object under test (built by `node tools/make_public_repo.js` on 2026-10-01, the run whose
thirteen fatal gates came back empty):
  nlcsplit-0.1.0-py3-none-any.whl  SHA-256 149e727be193ca8ddbead00e19979f713c2df7b15ad7b6f6dbed2a8bd22816ee
  nlcsplit-0.1.0.tar.gz            SHA-256 09ebcbd1942d1a68f95b98513d08bebca3c14913c57f4aa3615bfd7b3290ff65
  wheel members 36, of which six test modules plus conftest.py and __init__.py; zero .pyc.

Install: pip3 install --no-deps --target <extraction dir>, then PYTHONPATH=<that dir> with
the working directory set inside it, so nothing on sys.path is the repository.
`pytest --pyargs nlcsplit` therefore exercises the installed package, not the source tree.

Collected from the installed package: 45 = 27 under `-m "not slow"` + 18 under `-m slow`.
That is one more than each of the two wheels archived before it, which collected 43 and 44.
The two steps are two named tests in `nlcsplit/tests/test_partition_factors.py`, and the
file now holds six test functions where it held five at the previous commit:
  43 -> 44  test_soft_attribution_owner_masked_matches_analytic_gaussian (the owner-masked
            soft attribution against the analytic pi^{3/2} of the same Gaussian)
  44 -> 45  test_base_w_on_real_density_is_not_a_measure_at_all (the same base_w table fed
            the SCF density, where the implied factor is neither natm nor a constant)
The attribution referee round added no test to the shipped modules; its scripts live under
evidence/ and are data, not package code.

Environment: PySCF 2.12.1, Python 3.10.12 (WSL), numpy 2.2.6, scipy 1.15.3.
Result: 44 passed, 1 skipped, 322.21 s. The skip is the designed one - `test_geometry.py`
passes without the S22 official geometry download - and it is the only skip in the suite.

The export was run again after this test, which rebuilt the wheel to container hash
27aadd3c55c547c5ed5e1f8ba5c5d8f4346dc11343c941429f03d9d078269a59. All 31 packaged members
of that rebuild are byte-identical to the ones installed here (compared file by file with
SHA-256; zero mismatches, zero absent), so this run does describe the shipped content of
the newest build even though its container hash no longer matches. That is the point of
naming the hash: the container moves with mtimes, the packaged bytes did not move at all.

Wall clock is 322 s here against 406-656 s for the wheels that collected 43 and 44. The
extra test does not account for that - one test in 45 cannot explain a factor of two - and
the logs themselves give the reason: both earlier commands are recorded with
`OMP_NUM_THREADS=2` in their headers, while this run left the thread variables unset on a
20-core box, so its BLAS calls ran multi-threaded. The timings are therefore listed per log
and never pooled into one "wheel test time"; only the pass/skip counts are comparable
across them.

